---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import NavBar from "../components/NavBar.astro";
import { publicDomains, publicSkillCatalog } from "../data/publicSkillCatalog";
---

<BaseLayout title="ShipGlows Modes | The public métier catalog" description="The one-line ShipGlows catalog: one router, thirteen public métiers, and the modes that select their work.">
  <main class="page-shell">
    <NavBar />
    <section class="section"><div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Skill launch guide</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Describe the result you need, not the internal chain of commands.</h1>
      <p>Start with <code>shipglows &lt;instruction&gt;</code> when you do not know the owner. A public métier asks only what it cannot safely learn from context, then carries the work through implementation, proportionate proof, documentation impact, and closure.</p>
    </div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="pricing-grid">
      <article class="price-card"><p class="eyebrow">One question at a time</p><h2>Keep your attention for decisions that change the outcome.</h2><p>Product choice, public claim, permission, cost, security posture, or irreversible scope can need your decision. Implementation mechanics and internal handoffs are ShipGlows’s job.</p></article>
      <article class="price-card accent-card"><p class="eyebrow">A to Z ownership</p><h2>Let one owner carry the workflow.</h2><p>You do not have to choose the next internal step. Specs, readiness, checks, browser proof, research, and release gates remain available as expert tools, but the owning métier selects them when they are needed.</p></article>
    </div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="docs-panel"><div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Codex expert shortcuts</p>
      <h2>Use a short route when you already know the kind of help you need.</h2>
      <p>These shortcuts retain the same métier ownership: <code>shipglows explore|spec|status|resume</code> enters an expert <code>sg-planning</code> mode; <code>build</code> enters <code>sg-development</code>; <code>fix</code> enters <code>sg-bug</code>; <code>test|browser</code> enters <code>sg-engineering</code>; and <code>ship|deploy|prod</code> enters <code>sg-release</code>. <code>verify</code> preserves an explicit specialist owner. <code>core</code> alone switches the whole request to ShipGlows-system maintenance.</p>
    </div><div class="docs-links"><span class="docs-link"><code>alias → métier mode → internal engine</code></span></div></div></section>
    <section class="section" id="auto-mode"><div class="docs-panel"><div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Autonomous mode</p>
      <h2><code>shipglows auto</code> spends the session on useful work already grounded in the current project.</h2>
      <p>It selects substantial roadmap, planning, security, compliance, or architecture work without inventing busywork. The project root stays fixed, useful subagents are allowed, and the mode always implies <code>#nolocal</code>: implementation may advance, while builds, tests, installs, commits, pushes, deployments, and other external writes stay deferred.</p>
    </div><div class="docs-links"><span class="docs-link"><code>shipglows auto [scope] [until=HH:MM]</code></span><span class="docs-link"><code>shipglows auto #ci until=18:00</code></span></div></div></section>
    <section class="section" id="execution-posture-tags"><div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Execution posture</p>
      <h2>Modes choose the workflow. Tags choose where executable proof may run.</h2>
      <p><code>#local</code> permits proportionate local proof. <code>#nolocal</code> defers builds, tests, installs, runtime workloads, and external writes. <code>#ci</code> implies <code>#nolocal</code> and identifies existing CI as the deferred proof target; it never authorizes a push or workflow dispatch. <code>#local</code> conflicts with the other two, while <code>#nolocal #ci</code> is valid.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-secondary" href="/focus-tags#execution-posture-tags">See tag examples</a></div>
    </div></section>
    {publicDomains.map((domain) => {
      const skills = publicSkillCatalog.filter((skill) => skill.domain === domain);
      return skills.length > 0 && <section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">{domain}</p><h2>{domain === "Start here" ? "The router" : "Public métiers"}</h2></div><div class="role-list">{skills.map((skill) => <div class="role-row"><code>{skill.name}</code><span><strong>{skill.purpose}</strong><br /><span>Modes: <code>{skill.modes}</code></span></span></div>)}</div></section>;
    })}
    <section class="section"><div class="cta-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">sg-help</p>
      <h2>Use <code>sg-help mode</code> for this public map.</h2>
      <p>Use its expert-catalog option to see owner-bound shortcuts and the internal engine inventory. Numeric lifecycle and specialist engines remain real, but they are not default public choices.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="/skills">Browse métier pages</a><a class="button button-secondary" href="/docs">Read the workflow doctrine</a></div>
    </div></section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
